      "max_output_tokens": 2048,
      "model": "gpt-6.2-sol",
      "output": "{\n  \"choice\": \"within\",\n  \"base_and_horizon\": {\n    \"text\": \"Nintendo guided to JPY 200,000 million in consolidated profit attributable to owners of parent for the fiscal year ending March 31, 2021 (PDF p. 2). My base case is within guidance: the full-year actual falls no more than 2% above or below JPY 200,000 million, inclusive.\",\n    \"evidence_ids\": [\"P1\"]\n  },\n  \"causal_driver\": {\n    \"text\": \"The main driver is whether Switch production can catch up after temporary delays. Nintendo reported production and shipment delays in some regions but did not identify a specific component shortage (PDF p. 3). My base case is that any component bottlenecks ease soon enough for Nintendo to recover deferred hardware sales and for those buyers to contribute software sales before year-end, supporting parent-attributable profit. This is consistent with management’s assumption that full-year supply meets demand and planned titles ship on time (PDF p. 4).\",\n    \"evidence_ids\": [\"P5\", \"P2\", \"P6\"]\n  },\n  \"alternative\": {\n    \"text\": \"Lower: if component constraints persist, Nintendo could lose hardware sales rather than merely defer them. A smaller Switch installed base would also mean fewer opportunities to sell software during the fiscal year, potentially pushing parent-attributable profit below the tolerance band (PDF pp. 3–4).\",\n    \"evidence_ids\": [\"P5\", \"P2\", \"P6\"]\n  },\n  \"revision_signal\": {\n    \"text\": \"I would switch from within to lower if an interim update cut the full-year Switch hardware unit forecast because of component shortages. That would directly undermine management’s assumption that production can meet full-year demand (PDF p. 4).\",\n    \"evidence_ids\": [\"P2\", \"P6\"]\n  },\n  \"historical_comparison\": {\n    \"text\": \"The guide was 22.7% below the just-reported year ended March 31, 2020, using Nintendo’s published year-on-year comparison for the same profit metric (PDF p. 2). For supply-risk perspective, Nintendo said the production and shipment delays in February and March had only a limited impact on FY2020 results (PDF p. 3); that does not establish that a longer disruption would be similarly contained.\",\n    \"evidence_ids\": [\"P1\", \"P5\"]\n  }\n}",
